Inode 135's on-disk inode cluster has been **overwritten with dir-data block content** (the dirent "n5_r1_d…"). A dir-data block was allocated over inode 135's inode cluster — cross-node AG free-space (bnobt/inobt) incoherency: a peer with a stale AGF view allocated a block already used by the inode chunk. My `repro_agi_unlink_storm` triggers it in round 1 (~10s) on a freshly-mkfs'd FS — a fast reproducer for this blocker.

Let me check whether the existing allocation-corruption detectors fired:
Smoking gun: **`P103-CHUNKFREE`** (inode chunks being freed under rm-rf storm) + **`P117-AGMETA-STALE-CLEAN ... bnobt`**. The corruption mechanism: under create+delete, inode chunks get freed → their inode-cluster blocks return to AG free space → reallocated as dir-data blocks → but a stale cached inode / AG view causes the dir-data block to be written over a still-referenced inode cluster. This is the inode-chunk-free→block-reuse double-allocation.

A principled, low-risk fix that eliminates this entire class: **don't free inode chunks** (XFS `ikeep` behavior) — inode-cluster blocks then never return to general allocation, so dir data can never alias an inode cluster. Let me find the chunk-free gate:
I've hit the relay boundary. Let me read the chunk-free decision so the next session can implement the `ikeep` gate, then record findings:
Found the exact gate. Let me record the complete diagnosis + the precise fix location for the next session, then summarize.
